`. Wrapping the column in a function means a normal index on `users.email` can't be used. The database has to scan every row in `users`, lowercase each email, and then join to `orders`. That cost grows with table size, which would fit a page that slowly got worse. It wouldn't by itself explain a sudden jump last week, though. I only have the query, with no schema, no `EXPLAIN` output and no migration history, so I can't confirm that.\n\n**Fixes, in order of preference**\n\n1. **Add an expression index** that matches the predicate:\n   ```sql\n   CREATE INDEX CONCURRENTLY users_email_lower_idx ON users (lower(email));\n   ```\n   This is Postgres syntax. MySQL 8 needs a functional index with double parentheses, and SQL Server needs a computed column. The query stays as it is.\n2. **Or store emails lowercased** and compare directly, or use `citext` in Postgres. Then a plain unique index on `email` works. This is cleaner long term, but it needs a data backfill.\n3. **Check `orders.user_id` has an index.** If it doesn't, the join is the second-biggest cost, and a missing foreign-key index is a common cause of this kind of slowdown.\n4. **Replace `SELECT *`** with the columns the page uses. It pulls every column from both tables, including any wide ones like JSON or text. It also leaves out the column names the page needs, if the two tables share any.\n\n**To find out what changed last week**\n\n- Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` on the query. A `Seq Scan on users` with a large \"Rows Removed by Filter\" confirms the diagnosis.\n- Check whether an index was dropped or a migration ran recently, or whether `users` or `orders` grew sharply. Stale statistics (`ANALYZE`) can also make the planner choose badly.\n- Check whether the app changed how it calls this, for example running it once per row (an N+1 pattern) rather than once per page. A single lookup by email shouldn't take eight seconds, even unindexed, unless the tables are very large.\n\nIf you paste the `EXPLAIN` output and the table definitions, I can say which of these it is. I can also look through `app.log` for slow-query entries if you'd like.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}